--- Shader_01_Shaping/title/before
+++ Shader_01_Shaping/title/after
@@ -1 +1 @@
-Write a rule for each surface sample
+Give a familiar form a surface rule
--- Shader_01_Shaping/storyboard/before
+++ Shader_01_Shaping/storyboard/after
@@ -1 +1 @@
-At the five graph panels, the student follows a threshold and then a moving wave. They bring functions, sine, coordinates and colour mapping, then write one small fragment rule that reads supplied UV coordinates and assigns a colour. A named uniform exposes one input; step and smoothstep offer distinct transitions. The panel mesh, shader material connection and graph overlay are supplied. They leave with a usable surface shader and understand why reading TIME anew differs from advancing a stored phase, without retelling sine as if it had never appeared.
+At the graph panels, the student returns to the point, distance and the boundary of a face. Bringing coordinates, functions, colour mapping and the garment studio’s first shader, they make one circular mark from distance to a chosen centre. A threshold turns that distance into an inside and an outside; a narrow transition softens the visible edge. The existing graphs explain the rule. The panel, material binding and coordinate display are supplied. The student changes centre and radius independently, then explains why the mark moved while the panel did not. They carry a small mask function and its parameters. A band or box is a later variation; the timed graph remains a comparison, not a second opening lesson.
--- Shader_01_Shaping/storyboard_short/before
+++ Shader_01_Shaping/storyboard_short/after
@@ -1 +1 @@
-Use graph panels. Reuse functions, sine, coordinates and colour mapping. Teach a fragment rule reading UV and assigning colour, with one uniform; compare step and smoothstep. Supply panel mesh, material connection and graph overlay. Carry a usable shader; distinguish reading TIME from advancing stored phase. Open: settle the inconsistent FREQUENCY versus TIME-times-speed demonstration.
+Use graph panels to explain a circular mark. Reuse point distance, functions, colour mapping and garment UVs. Teach coordinate → distance → mask; expose centre/radius and compare sharp/soft edge. Supply panel/material binding. Check: move centre without changing radius; explain unchanged mesh. Carry mask + settings. Optional band/box; defer clock analysis. Open: learner binding; softened edge needs valid bounds.
--- Shader_05_Patterns/title/before
+++ Shader_05_Patterns/title/after
@@ -1 +1 @@
-Make a tile address inside every tile
+Transform the form inside its address
--- Shader_05_Patterns/storyboard/before
+++ Shader_05_Patterns/storyboard/after
@@ -1 +1 @@
-The tiled panel shrinks one shape into a repeated field, while the garden carries pattern across fixed flowers. Bringing array addresses, tiling and the earlier garment sampler, the student deepens shader coordinate use by separating a tile's integer address from its local fractional position. They use the address to choose a variation and local position to draw the same motif. The panel, shape-distance helper and garden receiver are supplied. The outcome is a reusable procedural pattern whose seams and phase can be explained without creating one geometry node for every mark.
+The tiled squares return to the earlier floor and garment, where a pattern already depended on how it was sampled. The student brings the retained mask, transforms and array addresses. They first offset and turn a band or asymmetric version of the mark around a named centre, then repeat it in a four-by-four study. The integer tile address chooses a variation; the fractional coordinate evaluates the form inside that tile. One chosen tile turns while the rest keep their orientation. They carry the mask, coordinate transform and address rule as separate parts. The garden offers a further comparison: its supplied world projection lets a pattern cross several bodies. Moving the learner’s function there needs an explicit adapter that preserves that projection.
--- Shader_05_Patterns/storyboard_short/before
+++ Shader_05_Patterns/storyboard_short/after
@@ -1 +1 @@
-Use tiled panel and garden. Reuse garment sampling, array addresses and fragment rules. Teach integer tile address for variation and fractional position for drawing one motif; inspect seams and phase. Supply panel, shape-distance helper with explicit inputs/output and garden receiver. Carry procedural repetition without per-mark nodes. Open: applying the learner's material to the garden remains proposed.
+Use tiled panels; return to floor/garment sampling. Reuse mask, transforms and array addresses. Offset/turn an asymmetric mark, then repeat 4×4. Teach floor = tile ID; fract = local coordinate. Check: turn one tile only. Carry mask + transform + address rule. Supply host; garden projection stays world-based. Open: binding/transfer adapter. Deferral: no new Shapes/Matrices halls.
--- Shader_11_QueerRubber/title/before
