--- Shader_11_QueerRubber/storyboard/before
+++ Shader_11_QueerRubber/storyboard/after
@@ -1 +1 @@
-Walking around the black ring, the student separates its moving rainbow rim from the lamp's highlight. They bring dot products and shader colour output, then meet the normal: a supplied vector describing the surface's direction. With normal and view direction normalized in the same reference frame, their dot product becomes a rim rule. The vectors, material host and lit exhibit bodies are supplied; a teaching material is proposed on an existing comparison body. They learn to compose view-dependent emission and carry a reusable rim material, distinguishing its response to the viewer from received light.
+The black ring catches a rainbow rim while the larger primitive bodies answer the lamps. The student returns to the faceted sphere, the garment’s curved receiver and the distinction between moving an object and moving around it. They compare one proposed teaching material on a sphere and a cube before adding a rim from supplied normal and view vectors in the same frame. Dot products now become an appearance. Keeping the material fixed, they change the body or viewpoint and account for the difference. They carry a material whose receiver matters. The existing leather, plastic and oil-like appearances remain available for looking and interpretation; the lesson does not require rebuilding all of them.
--- Shader_11_QueerRubber/storyboard_short/before
+++ Shader_11_QueerRubber/storyboard_short/after
@@ -1 +1 @@
-Use black ring's rainbow rim and lamp highlight. Reuse dot products and shader colour. Teach supplied surface normal; normalize it and view direction in one frame before forming a rim rule. Supply vectors, material host and lit bodies. Carry view-dependent emission distinct from received light. Open: teaching material on a comparison body remains proposed; no installed desks.
+Use ring and staged primitives. Reuse faceted sphere, curved garment, transforms, dot product and retained material. Compare one material on sphere/cube; teach rim from unit normal/view vectors in one frame. Check: predict changed rim with body/viewpoint changed and material fixed. Carry receiver-aware material. Supply geometry/vectors/lamps. Open: shared-material and hold controls absent; emission is not a lamp.
